Z||000003||Fret Naught||05-11-2000||01:54 AM||||No offense, but why? by the time you spend the money on a kit, modifications,paint, and various assundries, you probably could pick up a real nice low milage T.A. and get what you want. I guess that is one of the reasons I love the Fiero, it puts a sporty car with darn good stock performance (V6) in the average Joe's price range. Just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jhogans||05-11-2000||10:51 AM||||Fret,<P>I was just curious on what it would take.  You are correct I could probably buy a low mileage TA but that takes the fun out of building a kit car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spyder||05-11-2000||12:20 PM||||Sure it can be done,<BR>To start you need to make measurements and see if it will sit somewhere close  into place and then go from there. Like the fenders lining up or not, if they don‘t come close it might not be feasible. The angles on the car might not look right either. <BR>I don’t believe you would want to cut the urethane bumper but you can fill in gaps with fiberglass or metal. You’ll have to fabricate some brackets.<BR>Also there is the underneath the Fiero fascia, you might have to do some modifications there.<BR>Anything Is Possible, but like Haze said, Deep Pockets.<P>I am about to put the new front end of a 1998-00 Camaro on a 1997 Camaro, but putting it on the Fiero might be a bit harder.<BR>My friend had a small altercation with a ditch,  It jumped out in front of him.  So now he can get a new updated front end, and also going to add an after market ram hood. <BR>All that this install will take is the new front end, the new bubble looking headlights a new hood and some trimming  of the front fenders and modifying some brackets.  Then we’ll take it to a paint shop.||||reg||1
